> API and interpretation

The histories are actual row mutations. A standard-tableau interpretation
assumes distinct inputs. Capture stores copied row states and can exceed the
intrinsic tableau size; it has its own admission cap.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Schensted, Longest Increasing and Decreasing Subsequences (1961)|https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/canadian-journal-of-mathematics/article/longest-increasing-and-decreasing-subsequences/B5098D9BC8B226C575402B971852C05E]

